{"id":"5450d6fd-4210-4440-9582-e13da9bcc1c9","object":"chat.completion","created":1785037958,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    parent_to_children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            parent_to_children.setdefault(parent, []).append(node['id'])\\n    retained_set = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\\n    removed_order = []\\n    sorted_node_ids = sorted(node_by_id.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    for nid in sorted_node_ids:\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        if nid not in retained_set:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        children = parent_to_children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained_set for cid in children):\\n            continue\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            count = sum(1 for rid in retained_set if node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] == symbol and rid != nid)\\n            if count == 0:\\n                continue\\n        retained_set.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained_set)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task SE-PE-01. The case payload contains:\n- nodes: list of node objects with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n- required_symbols: list of symbols that must remain present.\n- _case_nonce: ignored.\n\nObjective: Perform grammar-guided node reduction (like Perses). The algorithm: Try to remove nodes that are removable, but only if they are not required symbol and not root, and only if removing them still keeps all required symbols represented. Also need to consider deeper nodes before shallower (so process by descending depth). Use node ID as tie-breaker for same depth. We must return two arrays:\n- removed_node_ids: order of removal (presumably the order they were removed).\n- retained_node_ids: sorted list of IDs that remain after reduction.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Need to build a tree structure from nodes. Nodes have parent references (by id). The root has parent null. Need to check if a node can be removed: \n- node.removable must be true.\n- node.id not root? root is likely \"root\" but check.\n- node.symbol not in required_symbols? But required_symbols is list of symbols, not node IDs. So we must ensure that after removal, each required symbol appears at least once in the remaining nodes.\n- Also, node must not have children that are retained? Actually the description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children, we cannot remove it until its children are removed first? But the algorithm processes deeper nodes first, so children (deeper) would be considered before parent. That aligns: we only remove leaf nodes? But the Perses reduction also allows replacement? Wait: \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" and \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we only remove nodes that are leaves in the current tree (no children that are retained). However, after removal, parent may become leaf later. So algorithm:\n- Build initial set of retained nodes (all nodes).\n- Build a map from parent id to list of child ids.\n- Sort nodes by descending depth, and for same depth, by id (ascending? Use stable tie break: \"use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So ascending alphabetical order? Typically \"stable tie break\" means alphabetical order of ID to ensure deterministic order. We'll use a custom key: (-depth, id) for sorting.\n- For each node in that order (from deepest to shallowest):\n   - if node.id is root? root id may be \"root\". Skip.\n   - if not node.removable: skip.\n   - Check if node.symbol is in required_symbols? But the instruction: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if node's symbol is required, skip. But note: there could be multiple nodes with same symbol; we only need at least one of each required symbol to remain. So we cannot remove a node if it's the only one providing that symbol. So check: after removing this node, would there still be at least one node (among retained) with that symbol? That's the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\". Also, for non-required symbols, we don't care about representation.\n   - Additionally, check if node has any retained children. Since we process deepest first, children would have been already considered and either removed or retained. We need to know current children status. So we need to maintain a set of retained node ids. Initially all nodes are retained. As we decide to remove a node, we remove it from retained set. When considering a node, we check if any of its children (original children) are still in retained set. If yes, cannot remove (since it has retained children).\n   - If all conditions satisfied, remove the node (add to removed_node_ids list in order of removal, and update retained set).\n- After processing all nodes in that order, remaining retained set are the retained nodes. Extract their ids, sort them (presumably alphabetically by id). The output requires \"retained_node_ids\" sorted. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted (how? likely lexicographically). And removed_node_ids in the order they were removed.\n\nEdge cases: root is never removed. Also, the root symbol \"root\" is typically in required_symbols? In examples