\" That's child check. Also \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That's depth order. \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Good.\n\nBut there is also mention of \"replacement candidates\"? But the task says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". However, the description of solve(case) only mentions removal? The fixture shows only removable bool and utility. But utility is not used? The problem mentions \"utility\" but the algorithm described does not use utility. Possibly utility is a factor in some version, but for this specific task, the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So utility is not part of the decision. So we ignore utility.\n\nAlso note: The problem might expect to consider replacement? But the return structure only asks for removed and retained nodes, not replacements. So we just do deletion.\n\nPotential nuance: The \"required_symbols\" list may contain symbols that are present in multiple nodes. For a required symbol, we need at least one node with that symbol to remain. That's correct.\n\nAlso, what about nodes that are not removable? They stay.\n\nTest with provided examples: We can mentally simulate the first case:\n\nNodes: root (id root, parent null, depth0, removable false, symbol root), n1 (parent root, depth1, removable false, symbol s1), n2 (removable true, sym s2), n3 (removable false, sym s0), n4 (parent n2, depth2, removable true, sym s1), n5 (parent n3, depth2, removable false, sym s2), n6 (parent n1, depth2, removable true, sym s0).\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1. So root is required symbol, s1 is required symbol.\n\nBuild children: root: [n1,n2,n3]; n1: [n6]; n2: [n4]; n3: [n5]; others none.\n\nInitial retained all.\n\nSort by depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Among them, id order: n4, n5, n6.\nProcess n4 (id n4, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1, depth2